  That turns out not to be the case.  Sting is nice early on, especially
in the mines.  Detect orcs when wielded is great.  If you can get to
Expert in daggers Sting is easily worth it in the early game so you can
survive to get a better weapon.  (Too much long term thinking can mean
you won't ever get to the long term.)


  At 6.0 base damage elven broadswords are almost the best one-handed
weapons in the game.  (Only the katana can match it due to better damage
vs large monsters.)  Elven broadswords are far more likely to turn up
early on. 

From art1-343.txt:

Orcrist
  (chaotic elven broadsword)
  Carried
    No effect.
  Wielded
    +d5 to hit and x2 damage only against orcs (all o, orc mummies, and
    orc zombies).
  Invoked
    No effect. 

Sting
  (chaotic elven dagger)
  Carried
    No effect.
  Wielded
    +d5 to hit and x2 damage only against orcs (all o, orc mummies, and
    orc zombies).
    Confers the warning intrinsic, specifically attuned to orcs.
    Cuts through all webs.
  Invoked
    No effect.


  Yes, they only do double damage vs a select number of monsters.  All
double damage artifact weapons are like that.  (Except Grayswandir.)

  Wielded Sting gives detect monsters vs all orcs.  2x base damage for
Orcrist means base 12 points of damage per hit vs orcs.  (That's a *lot*
of damage.  So much so that weapon skills don't matter.)  Between them
they can make orcs a pushover.  Wield Sting for early warning.  Maneuver
orc bands so they can only come at you one at a time, then switch to
Orcrist for the slaughter.


  For a gnomish healer in the mines they seem perfect, even though they
don't have any skill in broadsword.  (They do blast orc characters, I
believe.)

  Sting's ability to cut through all webs is also nice.

  For the late game, players ascend with Stormbringer.  Against many
late game monsters Stormbringer is worse than Orcrist, BION.  Ditto for
Fire Brand, Demonbane, Sunsword, etc..  (This is probably why no class
can get to Expert with broadswords.  They're just too good of a weapon.)


  So for early game survival they seem like a great combination.  Note
that if you don't survive the early game, mid- and late-game
considerations don't really come into it.  Note also that I've never
actually tried Orcrist early on.  I have tried Sting and it is nice.



